# Configuration

The server reads configuration from three sources, highest priority first: environment variables, a TOML file, then compiled defaults. Every value has a safe default except the ones a real deployment must supply itself: the database connection, the storage backend, and (in production) the signing key.

This page is the reference for the server itself. Database and storage each have their own page because their variables are read by separate crates: [Database](/self-hosting/database/) and [Storage](/self-hosting/storage/).

## Resolution order

1. Environment variables override everything.
2. A TOML file, resolved in this order and used if present: `$WYRD_CONFIG_PATH`, then `/etc/wyrd/server.toml`, then `~/.config/wyrd/server.toml`. If `WYRD_CONFIG_PATH` is set, the file must exist or boot fails.
3. Compiled defaults.

Configuration is validated after loading. An out-of-range value or a contradiction (for example the same address bound for HTTP and gRPC) fails boot with exit code `78`.

Field names and variable names refer to the same settings. Other pages name the config-file field (`shutdown.drain_ms`, `limits.timeout`); this page lists the environment variable that overrides it (`WYRD_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_MS`, `WYRD_HTTP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS`). Each table below pairs them.

## Deployment profile

`APP_ENV` selects the profile. `staging` and `production` both select the hardened profile; only `development` relaxes the gates. In the config file the same setting is the `deployment_profile` key, and `APP_ENV` overrides it.

| Variable | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `APP_ENV` | `development` | `development`, `staging`, or `production`. The hardened profile requires a signing key and gRPC reflection off. |

<Aside title="The hardened profile needs more than env vars">

A production or staging profile requires a real signing key; the open-source server ships a development-only ephemeral key, and `production_validate` rejects it at boot. Development profile has no such requirement.

</Aside>

## Bind and transport

| Variable | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_SERVER_BIND` | `0.0.0.0:8080` | HTTP listener address. |
| `WYRD_GRPC_BIND` | `127.0.0.1:50051` | gRPC listener address. Must differ from the HTTP bind. |
| `WYRD_GRPC_REFLECTION` | `false` | gRPC server reflection. Must be `false` in production. |
| `WYRD_SERVE_MODE` | `both` | `both`, `http`, or `grpc`. Overridden by `--mode`. |
| `WYRD_METRICS_ENABLED` | `true` | Serve the Prometheus `/metrics` endpoint. |
| `WYRD_METRICS_BIND` | auto | Defaults to `127.0.0.1` on the HTTP port plus one, so `/metrics` is not public unless you set this. |

## Peer mode

One process runs every Bifrost role in-process and needs no peer settings. To run several replicas against the same Postgres and object storage, set both variables below on every replica. Replicas find each other through Postgres membership, so they need no list of addresses. Split `oracle` and `scribe` targets require peer mode.

| Variable | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS` | none | `host:port` other replicas dial to reach this one, such as the pod IP and peer port. |
| `WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR` | none | Directory holding `ca.crt`, `tls.crt`, and `tls.key`: a dedicated cluster CA and one leaf certificate shared by every replica, with DNS name `wyrd-peer`. Keep the CA private key off the replicas. |
| `WYRD_BIFROST_PEER_BIND_ADDR` | `0.0.0.0:50052` | Private peer listener. Must differ from the HTTP and gRPC binds. Expose it only on the workload network. |

In Kubernetes, give every replica its own address from the shared pod template with the Downward API, never a per-pod value you write by hand:

```yaml
env:
  - name: POD_IP
    valueFrom: {fieldRef: {fieldPath: status.podIP}}
  - {name: WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS, value: "$(POD_IP):50052"}
  - {name: WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR, value: /etc/wyrd/peer}
```

A `forge-worker` process opens no peer listener. Give it `WYRD_PEER_TLS_DIR` alone so it can dial the Forge leader for compaction work; it refuses `WYRD_PEER_ADDRESS`.

The peer listener accepts only mutual TLS from that CA. A valid certificate identifies a cluster process, not a tenant: each receiver still checks the tenant, query, fence, and deadline of every call against its own state.

## Authentication

Every protected request carries a token the server signs itself, so a deployment supplies the material that makes those tokens work. There are three pieces here, and only the first is always required.

The **signing key** is Wyrd's own Ed25519 private key. It signs every token the server issues and verifies every token that comes back, so it is the one secret a production server cannot boot without. Lose it and nothing validates. Generating, mounting, and rotating it is covered in [Authentication](/self-hosting/authentication/#signing-key).

The **sealing key** encrypts IdP client secrets before they touch the database. "Sealing" is encryption at rest: when a tenant OIDC connection, the platform connection, or a workload issuer authenticates to its IdP with a shared `client_secret`, the server seals that secret with this key (AES-256-GCM) rather than storing it in plaintext. You need it only when such a secret is stored. Connections and workload issuers that use `public` or `private_key_jwt` client authentication carry no shared secret; a workload issuer with `secret_basic` or `secret_post` does, and needs the sealing key. Boot fails closed if a stored secret needs a sealing key and none is configured. See [Authentication](/self-hosting/authentication/#sealing-key).

The **public origin** and the **web-app client secret digest** enable human single sign-on; see [SSO and OIDC](/self-hosting/sso-and-oidc/#deployment-setup).

The **tenant slug** names the tenant boot-seeded workload issuers and bindings attach to. See [The implicit tenant](#the-implicit-tenant) below.

Signing and sealing keys are read from the environment only, never from the TOML file.

| Variable | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE` | none | Path to the Ed25519 signing key PEM. Required in production. |
| `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_PEM` | none | Inline PEM alternative, used only if `WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE` is unset. |
| `WYRD_PUBLIC_ORIGIN` | none | Origin browsers and IdPs reach Wyrd on; also `[auth] public_origin`. The provider callback is this origin plus `/auth/callback`. Required for human SSO. |
| `WYRD_UI_CLIENT_SECRET_SHA256` | none | Lowercase-hex SHA-256 of the web app's `wyrd-ui` client secret, or two comma-separated digests during a rotation. Without it, no authorization-code grant is accepted. |
| `WYRD_SEALING_KEY_FILE` | none | Path to a file holding a base64 32-byte AES-256-GCM key. Required only if a stored connection or issuer carries a client secret. |
| `WYRD_SEALING_KEY_BASE64` | none | Inline base64 alternative, used only if `WYRD_SEALING_KEY_FILE` is unset. |
| `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG` | none | Tenant that boot-seeded workload issuers and bindings attach to. Required if any is configured. Not needed for human SSO, which is configured per tenant at runtime. |

### The implicit tenant

Wyrd is multi-tenant by design, and a self-hosted deployment can serve one or more tenants. A protected request's tenant comes from its verified Wyrd access token, never from `Host` or another header. The only request-time tenant hint is for workload `jwt-bearer` issuance: the leftmost `Host` label, or else the form's `tenant` parameter, names a candidate tenant, which the external assertion and the server-owned workload binding must then verify. Boot has no request at all, so configured workload issuers and bindings need an explicit owner: `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG` names that tenant. It does not limit the deployment to one tenant.

You only need it when you federate identity. Trusted issuers and workload bindings are owned by a tenant, so the moment you declare either one (see [SSO and OIDC](/self-hosting/sso-and-oidc/)), the server has to know which tenant they attach to. Leave it unset if you configure no federation. If you do set it, the slug must resolve to an active tenant at boot; the server resolves it through the same lookup the request handlers use, so a bound issuer matches request-time identity. A slug that resolves to nothing fails boot rather than binding issuers to a tenant that does not exist.

## Gateway

The `[gateway]` table declares what tenant administrators may reference when
they [configure provider credentials](/how-to/administer-the-gateway/). It is
TOML-only.

| Key | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `credential_bindings.<name>` | none | An `env` (`{ source = "env", name }`) or `file` (`{ source = "file", path }`, absolute) secret reference. Tenants select `<name>` for an `environment` credential and never see the variable or path. |
| `secret_backends` | `[]` | External secret backend identities tenants may name in an `external_secret` credential. |
| `managed_secret_keys.<tenant-id>` | none | The tenant's wrapping keyring: `active` names one entry of `versions`, and each version is an `env` or `file` secret reference holding a base64 32-byte key. A tenant with no keyring cannot submit or resolve a `managed_secret` credential. |

```toml
[gateway]
secret_backends = ["vault"]

[gateway.credential_bindings]
openai-prod = { source = "env", name = "OPENAI_API_KEY" }

[gateway.managed_secret_keys.018f2c4e-7a1b-7c3d-9e5f-0a1b2c3d4e5f]
active = "v2"
versions.v1 = { source = "file", path = "/etc/wyrd/keys/tenant-a-v1.key" }
versions.v2 = { source = "file", path = "/etc/wyrd/keys/tenant-a-v2.key" }
```

Each version's value is read once at boot and held only in process memory;
Wyrd never persists key material. Boot fails, naming the tenant and version,
when a key is unreadable, is not standard base64, is not 32 bytes, or is
shared with another tenant — one wrapping key across tenants is not a valid
per-tenant configuration. Rotate by adding a version, pointing `active` at it,
and keeping the older version until nothing is sealed under it any more: new
submissions seal under `active` while committed envelopes keep opening under
the version that sealed them. Remove a version only after its envelopes have
been resubmitted; envelopes whose version is gone fail closed with the same
redacted upstream-unavailable error as a missing keyring, and never fall back
to another key, to plaintext, or to another source.

## Pools

| Variable | Default | Range | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_PG_POOL_MAX` | `32` | `>= 2` | Max pooled connections across the app, catalog, and recovery pools. |
| `WYRD_PG_POOL_ACQUIRE_MS` | `5000` | `100`–`60000` | Timeout to acquire a connection from the pool. |

## Request limits

| Variable | Default | Range | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_HTTP_BODY_LIMIT_BYTES` | `1048576` | `>= 1048576` | Max request body. Buffers up to this per request. |
| `WYRD_HTTP_AUDIO_UPLOAD_LIMIT_BYTES` | `26214400` | `>= 1048576` | Max gateway Audio transcription or translation upload. Streams to a temporary file instead of buffering. |
| `WYRD_HTTP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS` | `30000` | `1000`–`600000` | Per-request processing timeout. |
| `WYRD_HTTP_CONCURRENCY_LIMIT` | `1024` | `1`–`1048576` | Max in-flight concurrent requests. |
| `WYRD_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_MS` | `35000` | `1000`–`60000` | Drain budget for in-flight requests and verification runs on shutdown. The verification runtime drains for up to 30 seconds inside it; a budget below `31000` shortens that drain. |

## Verification runtime

Every API-serving process runs the verification runtime: one scheduler, the Verifier runner, and their durable queue in Postgres. Replicas coordinate through that queue, so no leader election is needed.

| Variable | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_VERIFICATION_ENABLED` | `true` | Run the verification runtime in this process. Set `0` to disable it or `1` to enable it. |

On shutdown the runtime stops claiming work immediately, gives in-flight runs up to 30 seconds to finish, then releases their leases so another process retries them under the same run identity. That drain happens inside `WYRD_SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_MS`, so keep your supervisor's stop timeout (Kubernetes `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`) above it.

### Operator connection keys

Wyrd stores every Slack, PagerDuty, and HTTP [Operator connection](/how-to/operate/operator-connections/) credential itself, encrypted in Postgres. Each credential version has its own data key, wrapped by a 32-byte key-encryption key (KEK). These variables say only where the KEK lives; credentials are never deployment configuration. Keys are read at use and never cached, so a rotated key file or Vault secret applies on the next read. They correspond to the `[verification.operator_keys]` config-file section.

| Variable | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_SOURCE` | `env` | `env`, `file`, or `vault`. |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_ACTIVE_VERSION` | `1` | Positive key version that new and rotated credentials are wrapped under, at most `2147483647`. |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_V<version>` | none | Base64 key for one version when the source is `env`. Development only. |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_DIR` | none | Directory of base64 key files named `v<version>` when the source is `file`. Each file must be unreadable by group and others. |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_VAULT_ADDR` | none | HashiCorp Vault base URL when the source is `vault`. Production requires `https`. |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_VAULT_MOUNT` | `secret` | KV v2 mount. |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_VAULT_PREFIX` | `wyrd/operator-keys` | Path prefix. Each tenant's key is read from `<mount>/data/<prefix>/<data_tenant_id>/<version>`, field `key` (base64). |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_VAULT_TOKEN_FILE` | none | File holding the Vault token, re-read on every key read and subject to the same owner-only check. |
| `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_VAULT_TOKEN` | none | Inline Vault token. Set this or the token file, not both. |

The production profile refuses the `env` source. A production deployment without `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG` is multi-tenant and must use `vault` over `https`, so each tenant has its own key; it fails startup if Vault or any active tenant's active key is unavailable. A single-tenant production deployment may use `file`. Outside those rules the server starts without a readable active key, and only creating a connection or changing its credential refuses, with `WYRD_OPERATOR_503_KEY_UNAVAILABLE`.

To rotate the KEK, publish the new version's key first, then raise `WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_ACTIVE_VERSION`. The server rewraps existing rows onto the active version in the background. Keep older versions readable until no row references them.

## Readiness

| Variable | Default | Range | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_READINESS_TICK_MS` | `5000` | `500`–`60000` | Interval between background readiness probes. |
| `WYRD_READINESS_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS` | `1500` | `100`–`10000` | Timeout per individual probe. |

## Telemetry

| Variable | Default | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `WYRD_LOG` | `info` | Tracing filter. Priority: `WYRD_LOG`, then `RUST_LOG`, then the config field. |
| `WYRD_OTLP_ENDPOINT` | none | OTLP collector endpoint. Setting it enables span export. Must be a valid `http`, `https`, or `grpc` URL with no userinfo, query, or fragment. |
| `WYRD_SERVICE_NAME` | `wyrd` | Service name reported on exported spans. |
| `WYRD_OTLP_PROTOCOL` | `grpc` | Only `grpc` is accepted via env. For HTTP OTLP, set `telemetry.protocol = "http"` in the config file. |
| `WYRD_OTLP_SAMPLE_RATIO` | none | Sampling ratio in `[0.0, 1.0]`. Unset means always sample. |
| `WYRD_OTLP_EXPORT_TIMEOUT_MS` | `30000` | OTLP exporter timeout. |

## Database and storage

These are documented in full on their own pages. In brief:

- Database: `WYRD_DATABASE_URL` is required and `wyrd-server migrate` runs first. `WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` is optional and defaults to it; production sets the two to separate tenant and platform logins you create. See [Database](/self-hosting/database/).
- Storage: set `WYRD_STORAGE_URL` (`file:///path`, `s3://bucket`, `gs://bucket`, or `az://account/container`). When unset, the server uses a local `.wyrd/storage` directory that other replicas cannot see. See [Storage](/self-hosting/storage/).

## Config file

Anything set by env can also be set in the TOML file. Two things are env-only: the signing key and the sealing key. Trusted issuers and workload bindings are the reverse: they are declared as TOML arrays, not env vars, and are covered in [SSO and OIDC](/self-hosting/sso-and-oidc/).

```toml
# /etc/wyrd/server.toml
deployment_profile = "production"

[http]
bind = "0.0.0.0:8080"

[grpc]
bind = "127.0.0.1:50051"
reflection_enabled = false

[limits]
body_bytes = 1048576
audio_upload_bytes = 26214400
timeout_ms = 30000
concurrency = 1024

[telemetry]
endpoint = "http://otel-collector:4317"
service_name = "wyrd"

[auth]
tenant_slug = "acme"
```

## A complete production environment

Each page above documents one slice. Here is the full set of variables a production deployment against external Postgres and an S3 bucket sets, in one place. Provision the two serving roles, run `wyrd-server migrate` with the owner login, and mount the signing key as a secret first.

```bash
# Profile
export APP_ENV=production

# Database. Both logins exist and `wyrd-server migrate` has run.
export WYRD_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_tenant:tenant-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd"
export WYRD_PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://wyrd_platform:platform-pw@db.internal:5432/wyrd"

# Storage (S3). AWS credentials resolve via the default SDK chain.
export WYRD_STORAGE_URL=s3://wyrd-artifacts
export AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Signing key (required in production).
export WYRD_SIGNING_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/signing-key.pem

# Operator connection keys. With no WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG this deployment
# is multi-tenant, which requires Vault over https.
export WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_SOURCE=vault
export WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_VAULT_ADDR=https://vault.internal:8200
export WYRD_OPERATOR_KEK_VAULT_TOKEN_FILE=/run/secrets/vault-token

# Observability (optional but recommended).
export WYRD_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4317
export WYRD_SERVICE_NAME=wyrd
```

This boots only if the deployment also provides a real signing key, per the note above.

## Validation rules

Boot rejects a configuration that breaks any of these:

- HTTP and gRPC must bind different addresses, and the metrics bind must not collide with either.
- Pool, limit, readiness, and shutdown values must fall in the ranges above.
- A production profile requires a signing key and gRPC reflection off.
- Any trusted issuer or workload binding requires `WYRD_SERVER_TENANT_SLUG`.
- `WYRD_OTLP_ENDPOINT`, if set, must be a valid URL with an `http`, `https`, or `grpc` scheme.
- Operator connection keys must follow the source rules in [Operator connection keys](#operator-connection-keys).

